    "description": {
      "type": "doc",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Yellowstone sits on a high volcanic plateau in the north-west corner of Wyoming, with edges reaching into Montana and Idaho. A supervolcano's caldera lies beneath it and feeds more than 10,000 hydrothermal features, including about half of the world's geysers.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "In 1872 Congress made Yellowstone the world's first national park. Old Faithful erupts roughly every 90 minutes, and the Yellowstone River drops 94 metres at the Lower Falls into a canyon of yellow rock. Gray wolves returned to the park in 1995 and now hunt elk across the Lamar Valley.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "text": "Most park roads open from late April to early November. June and September bring open roads with thinner crowds. Stay on the boardwalks in the geyser basins, where the crust over boiling water can be thin.",
              "type": "text"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
